What a “Pool”
A storage box that keeps already-created GameObjects so you can reuse them instead of creating new ones.
////////////////////////////
WHY pooling exists
Instantiate() is expensive.
If you use it many times per second → lag.
So instead of creating new objects every time, pooling does this:
  ----------------------------------->
Create object once → store it → reuse it → store again → reuse again

POOL BOX
┌────────────┐
│  Electron1 │  ← saved, deactivated
│  Electron2 │
│  Ring1     │
└────────────┘
Note:-
A pool reduces lag and reduces memory garbage creation by reusing objects instead of Instantiating/Destorying repeatedly.

What happens when we use pool.ContainsKey(key)?
-->
if (!pool.ContainsKey(key))
{
    pool[key] = new Queue<GameObject>();
}
✔ Step 1 — What does ContainsKey mean?
ContainsKey(key) simply asks: "Does this Dictionary already have this key?"
It returns:
true → key exists
false → key does NOT exist
✔ Step 2 — What is Dequeue()?
 -->Dequeue() removes the front object from the queue and returns it
 Step 3 — What is Enqueue()?
 Enqueue() adds an object to the back of the queue.
 Take → Use → Return → Take → Use → Return

